        lab: 'vector', labConfig: { topic: 'scaling', scale: 1 }, experimentPrompt: 'Use the rectangle-and-circle drawing in this experiment. Predict its coordinates and dimensions at scale factor two, then reveal the redraw. Explain why it can be reconstructed from geometry at the new size.',
        check: { prompt: 'Choose a representation for an editable map made of roads and labels, and for a detailed landscape photograph. Justify each choice.', answer: 'A vector is suitable for the map because objects and labels can be edited and scaled geometrically. A bitmap is suitable for the photograph because its pixel grid records complex colour and texture variation. The choice follows the content and required editing, not a claim that one format is always smaller.' },
        papers: [], takeaway: 'Use bitmap or vector representation according to the information, editing needs and required scaling.',
        trap: '“Vectors never use pixels” is false for their screen display. Their defining feature is the object-based source representation.',
        bridge: 'Pictures sample information across space. Digital sound represents changes across time.',
